NAME
    cpus - list processors available to scoped debug commands

SYNOPSIS
    cpus

DESCRIPTION
    Lists every processor published by the attached core along with its
    current program counter, description, supported debug features, and
    code memory space (if any).

    Multi-processor consoles expose each chip under its own name (for
    example, main CPU, SPC700 sound CPU, and cartridge coprocessors such
    as SA-1, SuperFX GSU, or NEC DSP on SNES). Scoped commands accept a
    processor name as an optional first argument:

        bp sa1 add 82D7        breakpoint on the SA-1 PC
        cov gsu on             coverage of the GSU instruction stream
        bt sa1                 call stack of the SA-1
        step spc over          step the sound CPU over a call
        eval gsu r14 > 100     evaluate against GSU registers
        regs spc               dump SPC700 registers
        disasm gsu             disassemble from the GSU's current PC

    Omitting the processor name targets the main CPU (the first processor
    in the list). The name 'cpu' always aliases the main processor on
    every core regardless of its architecture name (for example, 'm68k' on
    Genesis). The alias 'cop' selects the first cartridge coprocessor
    listed after the main processor (excluding SPC700).

CAPABILITY FLAGS
    Each listed processor reports which debug operations the core wires
    for it:

        bp      Mid-instruction breakpoint halts.
        cov     Executed-address coverage recording.
        bt      Call/return seam for backtrace inference.
        regs    Named register inspection via regs(1).
        setreg  Writable registers via setreg(1).
        disasm  Code space and ISA disassembler via disasm(1).

    Hardware without a hardware call stack (such as SuperFX GSU and
    SPC700) does not publish 'bt'. NEC DSP program addresses are word
    indices rather than byte offsets, matching the DSP's own PC.

HALTING
    Only one processor halts at a time. Resuming skips the breakpoint
    check only on the halted processor so it leaves its current PC while
    other processors remain armed. Mid-frame halts preserve remaining
    cycle budgets so resuming continues the interrupted frame.

EXAMPLES
    cpus
    bp gsu add 008010
    cov sa1 on
    regs dsp

EXIT STATUS
    0   Processor list printed.
    1   No target attached or session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    bp(1), cov(1), bt(1), step(1), profile(1), eval(1), regs(1),
    setreg(1), disasm(1), copflow(1)
